# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: errno-setvar-accepted-by-vendors -- (setvar "ERRNO" 0) works on AutoCAD and BricsCAD, clautolisp refuses it
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P2
#+TYPE: divergence
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: -
#+DATE: 2026-11-03

* Symptom

Found while running the error-processing catalogue probe
(error-processing-per-dialect.issue) under clautolisp 2.3.81:

#+begin_example
$ clautolisp --no-init --dialect autocad -x '(setvar "ERRNO" 0)'
; error: Sysvar ERRNO is read-only.
#+end_example

The same under =--dialect bricscad=. The probe resets ERRNO before every
case, with the usual idiom

#+begin_src lisp
(setvar "ERRNO" 0)
(setq r (some-call ...))
(if (/= 0 (getvar "ERRNO")) ...)
#+end_src

and clautolisp aborted it at the first case, while all three vendors ran it.

* Vendor evidence (measured 2026-11-03)

The catalogue probe runs =(setvar "ERRNO" 0)= OUTSIDE vl-catch-all-apply
before each of its ~505 cases, so any refusal would have aborted it at
case 0:

| product                        | (setvar "ERRNO" 0)      | next (getvar "ERRNO")                         |
|--------------------------------+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------|
| AutoCAD 2022 FR, accoreconsole | accepted                | 0, then set again by the next failing builtin |
| BricsCAD V25 fr_FR, Windows    | accepted                | same                                          |
| BricsCAD V26 fr_FR, macOS      | accepted                | same                                          |

Whatever the vendor documentation says about ERRNO being read-only, it was
not rechecked here; the measurement is what counts. The test
=cador/tests/sysvar-catalogue-tests.lisp: host-setvar-on-errno-signals-read-only=
asserts the opposite ("ERRNO is documented read-only on both vendors"),
which the measurement contradicts.

* To do

1. =--dialect autocad-*= and =--dialect bricscad-*= (any spelling):
   =(setvar "ERRNO" n)= writes the session errno, as
   SET-AUTOLISP-ERRNO does, and returns =n=, as the vendors do.
2. Under those dialects, emit a one-time portability warning (pjb,
   2026-11-03: "with warning"), e.g.
   : [errno-setvar] in <loc>: (setvar "ERRNO" 0) is accepted by AutoCAD and BricsCAD; clautolisp writes the session errno
   Its wording and silencing follow the [vl-catch-all-apply-arity]
   precedent (clautolisp-dialect-warnings.org): an UNDOCUMENTED tolerance
   that every engine has.
3. =--strict=: decide between keeping :sysvar-read-only and accepting with
   the same warning. The vl-catch-all-apply-arity precedent suggests:
   accept and warn.
4. Rewrite =host-setvar-on-errno-signals-read-only= to follow the
   dialect, add a test for the warning, and update the ERRNO entry of the
   spec (chapter 16, "Normative ERRNO code table" notes) and
   clautolisp-dialect-warnings.org.
5. Bump DEVELOP (version.lisp).

* Related

- error-processing-per-dialect.issue: the probe that found this.
- Spec SETVAR table (autolisp-visual-lisp-specification-draft.org:10072-10087):
  setvar ERRNO codes on both vendors.
